sn11.24:4.1 starts: “‘Bring anger under your control;
sn11.24:4.2 starts: Do not let your friendships decay.
sn11.24:4.3 starts: Do not blame one who is blameless;
sn11.24:4.4 starts: Do not utter divisive speech.
sn11.24:4.5 starts: Like a mountain avalanche
sn11.24:4.6 starts: Anger crushes evil people.’”
sn11.4:9.1 starts: “‘They call that strength no strength at all—
sn11.4:9.2 starts: The strength that is the strength of folly—
sn11.4:9.3 starts: But no one can reproach a person
sn11.4:9.4 starts: Who is strong because guarded by Dhamma.
sn11.4:11.1 starts: “‘He practises for the welfare of both,
sn11.4:11.2 starts: His own and the other’s,
sn11.4:11.3 starts: When, knowing that his foe is angry,
sn11.4:11.4 starts: He mindfully maintains his peace.
sn11.4:12.1 starts: “‘When he achieves the cure of both—
sn11.4:12.2 starts: His own and the other’s—
sn11.4:12.3 starts: The people who consider him a fool
sn11.4:12.4 starts: Are unskilled in the Dhamma.’
sn11.6:2.1 starts: “‘Avoid, O Matali, with your chariot pole
sn11.6:2.2 starts: The bird nests in the silk-cotton woods;
sn11.6:2.3 starts: Let’s surrender our lives to the asuras
sn11.6:2.4 starts: Rather than make these birds nestless.’
